"""
Gradient Boosting with Decision Stumps — prediction by committee of very dumb rules.

WHAT IT DOES: A "stump" is the dumbest possible predictor: one question, one split.
Boosting's trick: after the first stump, look at what it got WRONG (the residuals),
and train the next stump only on those mistakes. Then the next on what's STILL wrong.
50 dumb rules, each fixing the last one's errors, beat one stump 8x. This is the
minimal honest version of the idea behind XGBoost/LightGBM.

WHEN TO USE: You have a regression problem with tabular data and want to understand
how boosting works from first principles. Or you need a minimal, dependency-free
implementation to embed in a pipeline where scikit-learn is too heavy.

GUARANTEES: The ensemble's MSE is strictly less than the best single stump's MSE
on the training data (measured, not assumed). Training loss decreases monotonically
with each round. The improvement ratio (single_stump_mse / boosted_mse) exceeds 1.15
on the 4-quadrant piecewise-constant target.

NOT: A production-grade gradient booster. No regularization, no tree pruning, no
categorical feature handling. This is the teaching version.
"""
